\section{Why this exists}{

\code{predict_win_probability()} builds a one-row data.frame and calls
\code{predict()} on it. That costs ~32 ms per delivery, which is fine for a chart
and unusable for a pipeline: \code{cricinfo.balls} holds 940,985 deliveries, so
scoring the corpus one ball at a time takes about 8.3 hours. The model calls
were never the problem -- \code{calculate_projected_score_from_model()},
\code{calculate_innings1_win_prob()} and \code{calculate_chase_win_prob()} already
accept N rows and build a single \code{xgb.DMatrix}. The cost was entirely in the
scalar feature assembly around them, so this function reproduces that
assembly with vectorised equivalents (\code{fcase} for the phase and run-rate
branches, the innings split done by subsetting rather than by \code{if}).
}

